EXT. MOUNTAIN MONASTERY COURTYARD - ITALY - DAWN

Autumn, 1944. Mist clings to the cloister stones. A well in the centre. A bell with no rope.

SERGEANT WALTER PIKE (30s, weathered, steady) sits on the well's rim, cleaning a rifle with a rag. CORPORAL EZEKIEL "ZEKE" ROSS (20s, lanky, bandaged hand) stares at the hills. PRIVATE ANTONIO DELUCA (19, round-faced, local boy) peels a potato into a helmet. FATHER BENEDIKT (70s, white beard, patched habit) sweeps the stones. CAPTAIN HELEN MARSH (30s, army nurse, sleeves rolled) writes in a notebook on an upturned crate.

PIKE
Padre, there's no need for that.

FATHER BENEDIKT
There is always need. The leaves do not respect armies.

Pike smiles faintly and goes on cleaning. Zeke drifts over to the well. Deluca keeps peeling.

ZEKE
Quiet one this morning.

PIKE
The quiet's a favour. Take it.

ZEKE
You think they're coming back?

PIKE
I think the lieutenant said wait. So we wait.

MARSH
(not looking up)
Walter, tell Ross to stop pacing. It tires the patients.

PIKE
You heard the captain.

ZEKE
I'm not pacing. I'm walking.

MARSH
Then walk where I can't see you.

Zeke grins, holds up his bandaged hand in surrender, and sits beside Pike.

DELUCA
Sergeant, my mother makes this with garlic. Will the Padre have garlic?

FATHER BENEDIKT
The Padre has one head of garlic. It is for Sundays.

DELUCA
It is Sunday.

FATHER BENEDIKT
(a beat)
Is it?

Pike checks his watch. Shakes it. Winds it.

PIKE
Might be.

Father Benedikt sets down the broom and walks toward the kitchen. Deluca follows with the helmet.

ZEKE
Don't give him all of it, Tony.

DELUCA
I will give him what he asks.

ZEKE
(low, to Pike)
He thinks we're on holiday.

PIKE
Let him.

MARSH
Walter. Come here a moment.

Pike sets the rifle on the rim and crosses to her. She hands him the notebook.

MARSH (CONT'D)
Count them.

PIKE
(reading)
Eleven. Seven walking.

MARSH
Four can't. I need a truck by tomorrow.

PIKE
There's no truck.

MARSH
Then a mule. A cart. A door and four men.

PIKE
I'll ask the Padre. He knows every shed on this hill.

MARSH
Ask him gently.

PIKE
I'm always gentle.

MARSH
You are always tired. It sounds the same.

Pike nearly laughs. He hands back the notebook. Behind them Zeke has picked up the rifle and is turning it over.

PIKE
Ross.

ZEKE
Just looking.

PIKE
Looking costs nothing. Put it down.

Zeke sets it down. Pike and Zeke look at each other, then at the hills, then at nothing.

A BELL rings once from somewhere far below. They all stop. Marsh, Pike, Zeke. Even Deluca, in the kitchen doorway.

ZEKE (CONT'D)
That's the village.

PIKE
That's a goat with a bell.

DELUCA
(from the doorway)
It is not a goat. It is Signora Fiore. She rings it when the bread is ready.

Beat. Pike exhales.

PIKE
Bread's ready.

MARSH
(to everyone)
Then let us eat before somebody changes their mind.

They file toward the kitchen. Pike last. He picks up the broom, finishes the corner Father Benedikt left, and follows.
